 About Faculty of Allied and Healthcare Sciences 










 The Faculty of Allied and Healthcare Sciences is dedicated to preparing skilled healthcare professionals who support the delivery of quality patient care. Allied Health professionals are trained to provide essential diagnostic, therapeutic, rehabilitative, and emergency healthcare services. 


 The faculty aims to develop competent, compassionate, and ethically responsible healthcare personnel capable of meeting the evolving needs of society and the healthcare system. 






 History of the Faculty 


 Founding Authorization: The Government of Uttar Pradesh, through the Uttar Pradesh State Medical Faculty, granted permission to King George’s Medical University (KGMU) to start twelve Allied and Healthcare (Paramedical) Diploma Courses vide Letter No. 9573/14 dated 30 October 2014. 



 Inaugural Batch: The first batch of Allied and Healthcare (Paramedical) students was admitted in 2015. An organized administrative structure was established with designated Nodal Officers, Academic In-charges, and Clinical Coordinators for each programme. The faculty was initially allotted space in the Kalam Centre. 



 Program Expansion (2017–2019): The Diploma in Blood Transfusion Technician programme was introduced. 



 Program Expansion (2021–2022): The Diploma in Anaesthesia and Critical Care Technician programme was introduced. 



 Current Standing: At present, the faculty offers 14 diploma programmes supported by a robust academic and administrative framework, including Nodal Officers, teaching faculty, and technical staff dedicated to quality education and training. 



 Administrative Support: The Dean’s Office is supported by adequate administrative personnel to ensure the smooth functioning of academic and operational activities. 







 New Undergraduate Programmes (Academic Session 2025–26) 

 From the academic session 2025–26, the faculty has introduced three new self-financed undergraduate programmes: 





